(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 21-year-old man has mild breathlessness and fatigue two weeks after a viral upper respiratory infection. Troponin is mildly raised, echocardiogram shows mildly reduced function, and he is hemodynamically stable. Which is the mainstay of his treatment?","choices":["Supportive care","Septal ablation","Immediate biopsy","Transplant listing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This stable young man with mild symptoms, a mildly raised troponin and mildly reduced function after a viral illness has myocarditis, and supportive care is the mainstay of treatment. Treatment is directed toward the clinical scenario, adding vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, diuretics, antiarrhythmics or anticoagulation as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has had positive blood cultures for 10 days despite an antibiotic regimen that matches his organism's sensitivities, and he still has fevers. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Evaluation for surgery","Stop antibiotics and observe","Start full anticoagulation","Switch to oral therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ten days of positive cultures and ongoing fevers despite an antibiotic matched to the organism's sensitivities is infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotic therapy, an indication for surgery. Cardiothoracic surgery evaluates him; other indications are heart failure from valvular dysfunction, abscess and fungal infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. His son asks how serious this organism is. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["It carries a good prognosis","It rarely needs any surgery","It resolves within a week","It is the most lethal cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism in infective endocarditis, with mortality of about 30% in people who do not inject drugs, 50% in prosthetic valve endocarditis and 5% in people who inject drugs. His prosthetic aortic valve puts him in the highest-mortality group. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with frothy urine and leg swelling is found to have a moderate pericardial effusion. Which tests best evaluate the suspected cause?","choices":["Urine culture","Thyroid function tests","Albumin and urine protein","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Frothy urine and leg swelling suggest nephrotic syndrome, and albumin and urine protein are the tests in the effusion workup for that cause. The underlying etiology is usually uncovered through a careful history and examination, as his urinary clue shows. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy, an ejection fraction of 25% and breathlessness on minimal exertion despite optimal medical therapy has a left bundle branch block with a QRS of 0.16 seconds. Which treatment addresses the conduction problem?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Biventricular pacemaker","Single-chamber pacemaker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated, but his is symptomatic, with breathlessness on minimal exertion and an ejection fraction of 25% despite optimal therapy. Symptomatic block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is treated with a biventricular pacer, and dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy is itself a named cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118. Her electrocardiogram shows a new right bundle branch block. Which acute cause of the block should be suspected?","choices":["Pericardial effusion","Hypertensive crisis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure from a pulmonary embolism is a named acute cause of right bundle branch block. Five days after hip replacement, with sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118, she fits that picture, and treatment of the block depends on the underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which pair of drugs can cause pericarditis through drug-induced lupus?","choices":["Metoprolol and lisinopril","Colchicine and ibuprofen","Amoxicillin and azithromycin","Procainamide and hydralazine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Iatrogenic causes of pericarditis include trauma, radiation therapy and drug-induced lupus from procainamide and hydralazine. Through lupus, the pair connects to the autoimmune causes of pericarditis such as systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a permanent pacemaker has severe claudication, and an intervention is planned. His pacemaker system is an older one, implanted 20 years ago. Which imaging study should be avoided?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Duplex ultrasound","Magnetic resonance angiography","Conventional angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance angiography is contraindicated with pacemakers and intracranial aneurysm clips, so with his pacemaker it is the study to avoid. It is otherwise the alternative to computed tomography angiography in contrast allergy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm that now measures 5.9 cm. Repair is planned. Which approach is usually required for this location?","choices":["Surveillance every 3 to 5 years","Endovascular repair only","Watch until it reaches 6.5 cm","Open repair with root replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surgical repair is generally indicated for ascending aneurysms over 5.5 cm, and hers is 5.9 cm. An ascending thoracic aneurysm often requires an open surgical approach with aortic root replacement, whereas a descending one can be repaired open or endovascularly. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy returns with recurring ankle edema and increasing abdominal girth. She admits to eating canned soups and fast food most days. Which dietary counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Drink extra fluids with every meal","Restrict sodium to under two grams a day","Raise salt intake to protect pressure","No diet change is needed with drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her recurring edema and growing abdominal girth with daily canned soup and fast food point to her diet, and patient education in dilated cardiomyopathy includes sodium restriction to less than 2 g/day. The same education covers the medication regimen, follow-up and removing any offending source. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is examined by a student who hears a loud systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Respiration does not change it. Which bedside maneuver would soften her murmur?","choices":["Standing up quickly","Sustained handgrip","Valsalva maneuver","Amyl nitrite inhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases blood flow in the heart reduces outflow obstruction and softens the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Sustained handgrip increases afterload, and rapid squatting or passive leg raising increase preload, so each decreases the murmur, while respiration has no effect. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation at 146 with palpitations. She is alert, her blood pressure is 132/84, and she has no chest pain or breathlessness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous rate control","Immediate ablation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New atrial fibrillation at 146 is rapid ventricular response, and with a blood pressure of 132/84, a clear mind and no chest pain or breathlessness she is hemodynamically stable. Stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated with intravenous rate control, with anticoagulation planned for all. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What may the electrocardiogram show in aortic dissection?","choices":["Always low voltage","Always ST elevation","Diagnostic Q waves","It may be normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram may be normal in aortic dissection, so a normal tracing does not rule it out. The diagnosis rests on computed tomography of the chest and abdomen to delineate extent, magnetic resonance imaging, which is good but slower, or transesophageal echocardiography. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with multiple serious medical conditions is a poor surgical candidate. He had a transient ischemic attack and has a 90% symptomatic carotid stenosis. Which intervention is most appropriate?","choices":["Aspirin and observation alone","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Carotid endarterectomy","Digital subtraction angiography alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His transient ischemic attack and 90% stenosis meet the over-70% threshold for intervention in symptomatic patients. Because he is a poor surgical candidate, carotid angioplasty with stenting, most often through the common femoral artery, is chosen: it is less invasive and allows intervention in poor surgical candidates. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 77"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with dilated, tortuous leg veins has a duplex ultrasound confirming superficial venous reflux. Which vein is most commonly involved in this condition?","choices":["Popliteal vein","Femoral vein","Short saphenous vein","Great saphenous vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dilated, tortuous veins with superficial reflux are varicose veins, which result from venous reflux and venous hypertension. Superficial veins are involved, most commonly the great saphenous vein, with the short saphenous vein also affected. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has heart-failure-like edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. The differential is narrowed to constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy. Which laboratory finding would favor constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["A markedly high natriuretic peptide","A normal sedimentation rate","A relatively low natriuretic peptide","Positive blood cultures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No B-type natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes constriction, but the level is likely lower in constrictive pericarditis than in restrictive cardiomyopathy. With the differential narrowed to those two, a relatively low value tips toward constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman learns her electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.22 seconds with every beat conducted. She feels well and asks at what point this kind of delay usually starts to cause symptoms. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Once the PR exceeds 0.12 seconds","Once the PR exceeds 0.20 seconds","When a QRS starts dropping","Once the PR exceeds 0.3 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her constant PR of 0.22 seconds is first-degree block, and the vast majority of such patients have no symptoms. Fatigue, shortness of breath, palpitations and chest pain usually appear once the PR interval exceeds 0.3 seconds, the point at which a permanent pacemaker is also considered. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with atrial fibrillation is being discharged after treatment of an embolic leg occlusion. Which warning symptoms should prompt him to return immediately?","choices":["Dry, shiny skin on the shins","Mild ankle swelling in the evening","Sudden pain, pallor, coldness, numbness","Calf cramps after long walks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is a named embolic source, so he is at risk of another embolus. Sudden pain, pallor, coldness and numbness match the six P's of arterial assessment (pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia), the signs of acute limb ischemia, a vascular emergency needing immediate care. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a slow sinus rhythm at 38 six hours after an inferior myocardial infarction. She feels faint and her blood pressure is 80/50. Each narrow QRS has a normal P wave before it. Which drug should be given first?","choices":["Metoprolol","Diltiazem","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sinus rhythm at 38 with a normal P wave before each narrow QRS, faintness and a blood pressure of 80/50 is acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia; sinus node ischemia is a listed cause, fitting the infarction. Atropine is first line, followed by a temporary pacemaker if it fails. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with tamponade from a malignant effusion has a blood pressure of 76/48 while the team prepares for pericardiocentesis. Which measure best supports her in the meantime?","choices":["Oral beta blocker now","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Intravenous fluid resuscitation","Intravenous loop diuretic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At 76/48 while pericardiocentesis is prepared, intravenous fluid resuscitation is given in tamponade to increase preload and prevent right ventricular collapse. It is a temporizing, stabilizing measure; expedient pericardiocentesis remains the definitive treatment. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a first deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. Genetic testing shows factor V Leiden, an inherited clotting abnormality. Which component of the Virchow triad does this finding represent?","choices":["Venous insufficiency","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Venous stasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is an inherited hypercoagulable state, listed with prothrombin gene mutation and protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency. In the Virchow triad it represents a clotting tendency of the blood, separate from stasis or vessel injury. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with chest pain after a car accident that killed her sister has ST elevation and an elevated troponin; stress cardiomyopathy is suspected. Her pain has eased and she is hemodynamically stable in the emergency department. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge home today","Refer for transplant listing","Outpatient follow-up only","Admit to cardiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Even though her pain has eased and she is stable, Takotsubo mimics acute coronary syndrome with no initial reliable differences, so she is evaluated and treated on established acute coronary syndrome protocols and admitted to cardiology. Treatment is then empiric and supportive, and most patients completely recover. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"In a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which step comes before any drug?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion","A vagal maneuver","An echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In stable supraventricular tachycardia, the narrow complex tachycardias, a vagal maneuver is first line, before any drug. Adenosine follows if needed, may be repeated once, and may convert the rhythm or slow it enough to show P waves; synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has a painful, swollen right groin two days after a cardiac catheterization through the femoral artery. Ultrasound shows a blood-filled cavity outside the arterial wall, communicating with the lumen. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Femoral true aneurysm","Acute limb ischemia","Atheroembolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm is a communication between the arterial lumen and overlying connective tissue after arterial rupture, forming a blood-filled cavity outside the vessel wall. The common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, from laceration by the access needle, and pain comes from swelling or nerve compression. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which cardiomyopathy strategy applies specifically to adolescents?","choices":["Palliative care discussions when advanced","Cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators","Assist device or transplant consideration","Genetic screening for hypertrophic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The age-specific strategies for adolescents are genetic screening for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and activity restriction for high-risk individuals, with medication for symptomatic relief and an implantable defibrillator in select cases. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"In tachy-brady syndrome, which tachyarrhythmia is most common?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tachy-brady syndrome is the form of sick sinus syndrome in which a tachyarrhythmia alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or offset pauses. The most common cause of the fast phase is atrial fibrillation, which appears as an irregularly irregular rhythm without P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"For recurrent, colchicine-resistant pericarditis with an inflammatory phenotype, which therapy do the 2025 guidelines now support earlier in the course?","choices":["An interleukin-1 blocker","Immediate pericardiectomy","Intravenous immunoglobulin","Azathioprine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 2025 guidelines support anti-interleukin-1 therapy earlier in the course when there is an inflammatory phenotype: C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever or evidence of pericardial inflammation. For recurrent, colchicine-resistant or steroid-dependent disease, anakinra or rilonacept is the preferred agent. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with crushing chest pain develops a wide QRS tachycardia whose complexes vary in shape from beat to beat; his QT before the episode was normal. He is still talking and has a pulse. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Intravenous amiodarone alone","Observation on the monitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A wide tachycardia with complexes that vary in shape is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, and with a normal QT and crushing chest pain, acute ischemia is the likely driver. It is never truly stable, so he is defibrillated even with a pulse, then sent for urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a medial ankle venous ulcer has used compression, elevation and wound care for seven months and the ulcer has not healed. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Vascular specialist","Infectious disease specialist","Dermatologist","Physical therapist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonhealing ulcers are one of the reasons to refer to a vascular specialist. Healing can take 4 to 6 months with compression and wound care, and an ulcer still open at seven months needs further workup for underlying infection or other causes. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 35, 37"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man collapses while playing basketball and arrives with a very fast regular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70. A vagal maneuver fails, and adenosine slows the rate to reveal an irregular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, each with a delta wave (slurred upstroke). Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral metoprolol","Intravenous atropine","A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine has slowed the rate and revealed an irregular rhythm with wide complexes and delta waves, which is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. In that situation intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is never repeated; he is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70, so a drug is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How many points does an alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis subtract in the Wells criteria?","choices":["One point","Two points","Three points","Five points"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis (DVT) scores minus 2, the only negative item in the Wells criteria. Every other item adds 1 point, and the total sorts patients into low (zero or less), moderate (1 to 2) or high (3 or more) likelihood. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman taking a loop diuretic for heart failure has new palpitations felt in her neck. Telemetry shows frequent premature ventricular contractions. Which laboratory values should be checked first?","choices":["Troponin and lipase","Glucose and lactate","Potassium and magnesium","Thyroid hormone and cortisol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) and hypoxemia are the named causes of premature ventricular contractions, and her loop diuretic puts potassium and magnesium at risk. Palpitations felt in the neck are the classic symptom of these beats, which occur with or without structural heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who smokes and takes an oral contraceptive has mild calf aching. Her Wells score is low, and her D-dimer returns positive. What is the next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Empiric anticoagulation alone","Reassurance and discharge","Repeat D-dimer tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her Wells score is low, so she started with a D-dimer, and a positive result leads on to ultrasound. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific, so it cannot confirm a clot, and venous duplex ultrasound has replaced contrast venography for diagnosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 56, 57 (slide 56 flowchart picture)"},{"q":"Which electrolyte is given as an intravenous push and then as an infusion to break torsades de pointes?","choices":["Magnesium","Phosphate","Potassium","Calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium is given to break torsades de pointes, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. Amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided because they prolong the QT, and lidocaine is the only antiarrhythmic that can be used. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man has an incidental finding on a preoperative electrocardiogram: a constant PR interval in conducted beats with occasional P waves that are not followed by a QRS complex, and no PR lengthening before the dropped beats. He has no symptoms. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance and follow-up","Repeat the tracing in a year","Start an oral beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A constant PR in conducted beats with occasional dropped QRS complexes and no PR lengthening is Mobitz type II. It is considered dangerous even without symptoms because of its unpredictable progression and poor prognosis, with a higher risk of complete block and sudden cardiac death, so the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has new palpitations. Her pulse is irregularly irregular and the electrocardiogram shows atrial fibrillation. Echocardiogram shows left atrial enlargement. Which treatment prevents embolic stroke in her?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Nitrate therapy","Anticoagulation","Digoxin for rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, atrial fibrillation, as in this woman with an irregularly irregular pulse and left atrial enlargement, is treated with anticoagulation to prevent embolic stroke. Antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone convert and suppress it, and radiofrequency ablation is another option. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which group has the highest incidence of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Asian men","Hispanic women","African American women","Caucasian men"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Caucasian men have the highest incidence of abdominal aortic aneurysm. Men outnumber women 2:1 under age 80, evening out to 1:1 over 80, and aneurysm is uncommon in African American, Asian and Hispanic populations. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with end-stage dilated cardiomyopathy is listed for cardiac transplantation, but his breathlessness and fatigue worsen despite full medical therapy and a biventricular defibrillator. Which device strategy fits his situation?","choices":["Pacemaker for isolated heart block","Assist device as a bridge to transplant","Alcohol ablation of the septum","Assist device as destination therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is already listed for transplantation and worsening despite full therapy and a biventricular defibrillator. A left ventricular assist device (LVAD), a pump that helps the left ventricle in heart failure, is indicated as bridge therapy for cardiac transplantation or while waiting for cardiac function to return. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with right lower lobe pneumonia has sharp chest pain. A rubbing sound is heard over the lower chest that is present only while she breathes in and vanishes when she holds her breath. Which is the most likely source of the sound?","choices":["The mitral valve","The pleura","The pericardium","The chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her rub is heard only during inspiration and vanishes with breath-holding, the pattern of a pleural rub, which fits her right lower lobe pneumonia. A pericardial rub behaves differently: its intensity increases when the patient holds a breath, leans forward or the diaphragm is pressed firmly. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever, a new murmur and positive blood cultures is stable. A transthoracic study is limited by his body habitus. Which imaging test best detects vegetations and defines the extent of disease?","choices":["Transesophageal echocardiogram","Chest radiograph","Repeat transthoracic study","Coronary angiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is highly sensitive and specific, detects vegetations on the valve leaflets and defines the extent of disease. With his transthoracic study limited by body habitus, it is the test to order, although a normal result does not necessarily rule out endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What causes congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation alone","Valvular dysfunction","Fluid from antibiotics","Coronary vasospasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis is due to valvular dysfunction: infection damages the valve, producing a new murmur, and can rupture the chordae tendineae. A new murmur together with heart failure points to a failing infected valve. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to anticoagulation for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Extensive proximal clot","Active cancer","A recent long flight","Acute intracranial hemorrhage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contraindications to anticoagulants are active bleeding, acute intracranial hemorrhage, major trauma and severe bleeding disorders. Anticoagulation is otherwise the mainstay of treatment for deep vein thrombosis (DVT), so a bleeding problem is what stops it. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with peripheral artery disease has a hard, dry, black distal toe with a clear line between healthy and dead tissue. There is no swelling, moisture or fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atheroembolism","Wet gangrene","Dry gangrene","Gas gangrene"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dry gangrene has a hard, dry texture, usually involves the distal toes, shows clear demarcation between healthy and necrotic tissue, and is common in patients with peripheral artery disease. His toe matches every feature, with no swelling, moisture or fever to suggest infection. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. His family asks what to expect. Which statement about his long-term outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Survival without treatment is usually normal","His age and comorbidities predict a worse outcome","His prognosis is as good as a young patient's","Age does not influence the outcome of surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Older age, worse functional class and comorbidities such as renal dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia are independent predictors of poor long-term outcome in constrictive pericarditis. At 78 with kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia, he carries several of them. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man taking apixaban for deep vein thrombosis develops an acute intracranial hemorrhage. Which agent reverses the anticoagulant?","choices":["Vitamin K by mouth","Andexanet alfa","Idarucizumab","Protamine sulfate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is taking apixaban and has an acute intracranial hemorrhage, one of the situations that calls for reversal. Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) is the reversal agent for apixaban and rivaroxaban, and it is used off-label for edoxaban. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which valve-related condition warrants endocarditis prophylaxis before dental work?","choices":["A history of rheumatic fever","A pacemaker without valve disease","A transcatheter prosthetic valve","Sinus rhythm with a flow murmur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prosthetic cardiac valves, including transcatheter-implanted prostheses and homografts, are on the list for which prophylaxis is reasonable before dental procedures that manipulate gingival tissue or the periapical region of teeth or perforate the oral mucosa. A transcatheter valve counts the same as a surgical one. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a native mitral valve has 5 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue and a new murmur. Three sets of blood cultures have just been drawn, and the organism is not yet known. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Oral amoxicillin, single dose","Nafcillin plus rifampin","Oral doxycycline for 2 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With the organism unknown, empiric therapy must cover staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci. For a native valve with a subacute presentation (5 weeks of low-grade fever), the empiric regimen is vancomycin plus ceftriaxone, started only after blood cultures are drawn, as they have been here. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy is found to have cardiac amyloidosis, with amyloid protein deposited in the heart tissue. He has breathlessness, leg edema and ascites. Which electrocardiogram finding is expected with this infiltrative disease?","choices":["ST elevation","Left axis deviation","Low QRS voltage","Prominent Q waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram in restrictive cardiomyopathy depends on the stage and diagnosis, and it may show low QRS voltage in amyloidosis infiltrative disease. Rhythm disorders such as atrial fibrillation and conduction disorders such as bundle branch blocks are also common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has frequent, disruptive episodes of atrioventricular nodal reentry and wants a definitive cure. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Septal myectomy","Permanent pacemaker","Valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For a select subset of patients with nodal reentry, such as this woman with frequent, disruptive episodes, catheter ablation offers the opportunity for a definitive cure, in over 95 percent. Acute episodes are otherwise ended with vagal maneuvers or adenosine, with a pill-in-the-pocket beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker as an option. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman collapses in a hospital hallway. The monitor shows chaotic, uncoordinated activity, and she has no pulse. Compressions have started and defibrillator pads are attached. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Check electrolytes first","Amiodarone before any shock","Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillate now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chaotic, uncoordinated activity with no pulse is ventricular fibrillation, treated as cardiac arrest. With compressions underway and pads attached, she is defibrillated (unsynchronized cardioversion) as soon as possible, followed by 2 minutes of cardiopulmonary resuscitation and epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man began having pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pericardial pain 8 weeks ago. Despite treatment the symptoms have never fully gone away, with no symptom-free interval. Which term best describes his course?","choices":["Incessant pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive disease","Chronic pericarditis","Recurrent pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His symptoms have persisted for 8 weeks, beyond the 4 to 6 weeks of acute pericarditis but short of 3 months, with no symptom-free interval. Symptoms persisting more than 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission define incessant pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant born at 28 weeks is in the neonatal intensive care unit with a central venous catheter and parenteral nutrition. She develops feeding difficulty, tachycardia, hypotension and a new murmur, and echocardiography shows a right-sided vegetation. Which organism group is particularly tied to her risk factors?","choices":["Gram-negative coccobacilli","Viridans streptococci","Candida species","Enterococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neonatal fungal endocarditis is tied to central venous catheters, hyperalimentation (her parenteral nutrition), neonatal intensive care and prematurity, and she has all four. Neonatal disease is often catheter-related and right-sided, like her vegetation; Staphylococcus aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci are the other common organisms. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man ran out of his metoprolol four days ago. He feels his heart racing, and his pulse is 118 and regular, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. He has no fever and is not bleeding. Which is the most likely cause of his tachycardia?","choices":["Raised intracranial pressure","Hypothyroidism","Increased vagal tone","Beta blocker withdrawal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A regular rate of 118 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus tachycardia, and abrupt withdrawal of a beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is a listed drug cause. Running out of metoprolol four days ago, with no fever or bleeding to explain the rate, fits that cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman taking digoxin for heart failure has nausea and fatigue. Her heart rate is 48 and regular, with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted) just after each QRS in II, III and aVF. Which is the most likely cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Digoxin toxicity","Hyperthyroidism","An accessory pathway","Missed digoxin doses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A slow, regular, narrow rhythm at 48 with inverted P waves just after each QRS in II, III and aVF is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause. Her nausea and fatigue while taking digoxin point to toxicity, which is treated with atropine and/or digoxin immune Fab (fragment antigen-binding). — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 9 days after hip surgery. She develops a new fever, and two sets of blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus; she has no indwelling catheter, and examination finds no wound or other source. Which diagnosis must be suspected?","choices":["Contamination of both culture sets","Postoperative atelectasis","Health care-associated endocarditis","Drug fever from antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has been in the hospital for 9 days and now has fever with two sets of positive blood cultures and no apparent source (no catheter, no wound). Health care-associated endocarditis is less common but should be suspected in exactly this situation, and 25-35% of native valve endocarditis is health care-associated. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been hospitalized for four days receiving intravenous antibiotics through a peripheral catheter in her left forearm. She now has pain, redness and warmth along the course of the vein, and a firm, tender, palpable cord can be felt beneath the skin. She is afebrile, and the redness stays within the line of the vein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis","Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain, redness and warmth along the course of a superficial vein with an indurated, palpable cord after recent catheter use is superficial thrombophlebitis. She is afebrile and the redness stays within the line of the vein, so nothing suggests septic thrombophlebitis; recent intravenous catheter use is the most common cause in the arm. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has had painful blue toes for several days after atheroemboli from proximal plaques. Which complication may follow in the affected digits?","choices":["Digital necrosis and gangrene","Rupture of the popliteal artery","Hoarseness from nerve stretch","Heart block from nodal fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her painful blue toes after atheroemboli reflect digital vascular occlusion causing ischemia, the blue toe syndrome. Digital necrosis and gangrene may develop, and localized tenderness, pallor and livedo reticularis occur at the sites of emboli. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman being evaluated for endocarditis had a soft systolic murmur documented for 10 years. Today the murmur sounds a little louder. How does this change count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["It is an immunologic phenomenon","It does not meet the major criterion","It confirms definite endocarditis","It meets the major criterion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New valvular regurgitation counts as evidence of endocardial involvement, a major criterion, but an increase or change in a preexisting murmur is specifically not sufficient. Her soft systolic murmur has been documented for 10 years, so a slightly louder version of it does not count. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man underwent cardiac catheterization through the right femoral artery and vein ten days ago. He now has a continuous machine-like murmur over the puncture site with a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the skin), and his right leg is swollen with prominent superficial veins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis of the leg","Iatrogenic arteriovenous fistula","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Femoral pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Femoral artery and vein access for catheterization, followed by a continuous murmur and thrill over the puncture site, points to an iatrogenic arteriovenous (AV) fistula. Fistula flow gives a thrill and bruit with both systolic and diastolic components; these fistulas most commonly affect the femoral vessels, and duplex ultrasound confirms the diagnosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction 3 weeks ago. He now has fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, three-part rubbing sound) at the left sternal border. Which drug should be avoided in treating his pericarditis?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Colchicine","Pantoprazole","Aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever, pleuritic pain eased by sitting forward and a rub 3 weeks after an anterior infarction make this post-infarction pericarditis. Nonsteroidal drugs such as ibuprofen are not given for post-infarction pericarditis, and in patients on aspirin as an antiplatelet agent, ibuprofen and indomethacin are avoided. Aspirin plus colchicine is used. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
